      <workList xml:id="workDesc_4bcb6f07">
         <work xml:id="work_id64638820">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_ecd50361">C7:001</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_id37b7b0ee" xml:lang="de">Die Allerneueste erfundene Art, nach Morgenländischer Weise, mit guter Manier ein Böses Weib los zu werden</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_8f432efb"/>
            <creation xml:id="creation_id6b6abc54">
               <date isodate="1753" xml:id="date_a4fd5462">1753</date>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_id168357cb">
               <p xml:id="idm85">Following the satirical essay on <rend xml:id="idm86" fontstyle="italic">Misogynis</rend> (that is, the women hater) published in 1750 (SchW C3:002), the present one was issued three years later. In a note to the third edition of <rend xml:id="idm87" fontstyle="italic">Misogynis</rend> which appeared in 1764, Scheibe disassociates himself from the present work. The second title page proves that this is a new edition of <rend xml:id="idm88" fontstyle="italic">Die böse Sieben</rend> (Wittemberg, 1662), the authors of which were connected to the German order ‘Elbschwanen’ that was a language and poetry society established in the late seventeenth century in Hamburg; Scheibe does not seem to have been a member. The afterword is by one of its members, <persName xml:id="idm89" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Johann Georg Müller</persName> (or Möller), also known as ‘Thromylas’. The name of the author does not appear on the title page but according to Holzmann &amp; Bohatta, it is Scheibe.</p>
            </history>
            <langUsage xml:id="langUsage_e4d592cc">
               <language xml:id="en">English</language>
               <language xml:id="de">German</language>
            </langUsage>
